---
title: Prompt page
layout: home
description: One prompt, the whole conversation it produced, and the paragraph explaining which line does the work. The window is the page.
eyebrow: Collections · Prompts
source: src/components/chat.css
---
<section class="im-section im-wrap im-pt-sm im-section-sm">
	<p class="im-eyebrow">Collections · Prompts · {{page.title}}</p>
	<p class="im-lede">{{page.description}}</p>
	<p class="doc-source"><code>{{page.source}}</code></p>
</section>

<div class="im-single im-wrap-wide" data-sidebar="on">
	<article class="im-stack im-stack-md">
		{{#with demo.prompts.[1]}}
		<nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
			<ol class="im-breadcrumb im-breadcrumb-chevron">
				<li><a href="/">{{> "icons/house"}}<span>Home</span></a></li>
				<li><a href="/collections/prompts/">{{> "icons/sparkles"}}<span>Prompts</span></a></li>
				<li><span aria-current="page">{{name}}</span></li>
			</ol>
		</nav>

		<header class="im-stack im-stack-xs">
			<p class="im-snipcard-meta"><span class="im-kind" data-kind="tag">{{> "icons/sparkles"}}Prompt</span><span>{{model}}</span><span>{{tokens}}</span></p>
			<h1 class="im-collhead-title">{{name}}</h1>
			<p class="im-lede im-m0">{{text}}</p>
		</header>

		<div class="im-chat">
			<p class="im-chat-model">{{> "icons/sparkles"}}{{model}}<span class="im-chat-model-push">{{tokens}}</span></p>
			<ol class="im-chat-log">
				<li class="im-chat-turn" data-from="you">
					<span class="im-chat-mark">You</span>
					<div class="im-chat-bubble">{{body}}</div>
				</li>
				<li class="im-chat-turn" data-from="model">
					<span class="im-chat-mark">AI</span>
					<div class="im-chat-bubble">{{reply}}</div>
				</li>
				<li class="im-chat-turn" data-from="you">
					<span class="im-chat-mark">You</span>
					<div class="im-chat-bubble">Now do the same for the section under it.</div>
				</li>
				<li class="im-chat-turn" data-from="model">
					<span class="im-chat-mark">AI</span>
					<div class="im-chat-bubble"><span class="im-chat-typing" aria-label="Thinking"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span></div>
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p class="im-chat-input"><span class="im-chat-caret">Ask a follow-up…</span><span class="im-btn im-btn-sm im-btn-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{> "icons/arrow-up"}}</span></p>
		</div>
		{{/with}}

		<section class="im-content im-content-flush">
			<h2>Which line does the work</h2>
			<p><strong>"Do not summarise. Do not praise."</strong> Without those five words every model on earth opens with a paragraph about how well-structured the document is, and the list that follows is shorter because the praise used the attention.</p>
			<p><strong>"Shortest first."</strong> An ordering instruction is the cheapest quality control there is: sorting forces the model to compare the items against each other, and the weak ones become obvious to it while it is sorting.</p>
			<p><strong>The word MISSING in capitals.</strong> Not because capitals are magic — because the prompt is otherwise a review prompt, and every review prompt in the training data ends in a summary. The capital is what stops it drifting back to the average.</p>

			<h2>Where it fails</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>On a document under about 300 words</strong> it invents gaps. There is nothing missing from a paragraph, and it will find four things anyway.</li>
				<li><strong>On a specification with numbered requirements</strong> it lists the numbers you skipped rather than the assumptions you made. Ask for assumptions explicitly if that is what you want.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>What I changed, and when</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>September 2026</strong> — added "shortest first". The lists got noticeably better.</li>
				<li><strong>June 2026</strong> — dropped "act as a technical reviewer". It changed nothing except the length of the prompt.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="im-section im-section-tight">
			<h2 class="im-caption">Others like it</h2>
			<div class="im-promptcards" style="--im-promptcards-min: 22rem">
				{{#each demo.prompts}}{{#match @index "!=" 1}}{{> "components/prompt-card"}}{{/match}}{{/each}}
			</div>
		</section>
	</article>

	<aside>
		<div class="im-sticky-rail">
			<div class="im-widgets">
				{{#with demo.author}}{{> "components/author" status="Open to work"}}{{/with}}

				<section class="im-widget" aria-labelledby="pp-facts">
					<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="pp-facts">About this one</h2>
					<dl class="im-widget-facts">
						<dt>Model</dt><dd>Reasoning</dd>
						<dt>Length</dt><dd>~90 tokens</dd>
						<dt>Written</dt><dd>March 2026</dd>
						<dt>Last revised</dt><dd>September 2026</dd>
						<dt>Used</dt><dd>Weekly</dd>
					</dl>
				</section>

				<section class="im-widget" aria-labelledby="pp-tools">
					<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="pp-tools">Runs well on</h2>
					<ul class="im-widget-list">
						<li><a href="#prompts">A reasoning model<small>What it was written for</small></a></li>
						<li><a href="#prompts">A fast general model<small>Works, shorter lists</small></a></li>
						<li><a href="#prompts">A local 8B<small>Needs the capitals</small></a></li>
					</ul>
				</section>

				<section class="im-widget im-widget-fill" aria-labelledby="pp-sub">
					<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="pp-sub">New ones</h2>
					<p class="im-widget-heading">Rarely, and only when one has earned it.</p>
					{{> "components/subscribe" id="prompt-email" button="Tell me"}}
				</section>
			</div>
		</div>
	</aside>
</div>

<section class="im-section im-wrap">
	<div class="im-prose">
		<h2>A prompt printed as a paragraph is not a prompt</h2>
		<p>It is prose <em>about</em> a prompt, and a reader has to work out where it starts and stops. Printed as the turn it is — in a window, with a reply under it — three things are obvious without reading a word: which part gets pasted, how long it is, and what it produced.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th></th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>im-chat</code></td><td>The window. Ordinary surfaces, radii and type from this system — no brand name, no logo, no borrowed colour. Nobody owns the idea of a message in a box.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-chat-turn[data-from]</code></td><td><code>you</code> or <code>model</code>. The prompt is the one bubble with a border, because it is the one a reader is looking for.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>data-trail</code></td><td>On a reply in a <em>card</em>: it fades rather than being cut, so nobody reads to the end expecting an answer.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-chat-model</code></td><td>Which model, at the top. A prompt tuned on one is not the same prompt on another, and pretending otherwise is how a prompt collection stops being useful.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-chat-typing</code></td><td>Three dots. Stops under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>

		<h2>The composer is a picture, not a form</h2>
		<p>A text field nobody can submit is worse than no text field. The bottom row says "this is where the text goes" and nothing else — it has no <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>, takes no focus and is out of the tab order. A page that really chats puts a real form there.</p>

		<h2>The explanation is the page</h2>
		<p>The prompt is four lines and takes ten seconds to read. <strong>Which line does the work</strong>, <strong>where it fails</strong> and <strong>what changed and when</strong> are the parts nobody else can write, and they are the reason this is a collection rather than a text file.</p>
	</div>
</section>
